      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term",
      "problem": "The coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ in the expansion of ${(2+x)}^{15}$ is",
      "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+x)}^{15},$ we have $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{15}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{0}){2}^{15}{x}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{1}){2}^{15-1}{x}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{2}){2}^{15-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{15}){2}^{15-15}{x}^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{12}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{12}){2}^{15-12}{x}^{12}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{12}){2}^{15-12} & =455\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =455\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3\\,640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ is $3\\,640.$",
